WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our first guest tonight is a talented actor

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.501
you know from the "Magic Mike" franchise,

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:05.934
as well as shows like "True Blood."

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:07.868
You can see him in "Metal Lords,"

00:00:07.868 --> 00:00:09.734
which premieres this Friday on Netflix.

00:00:09.734 --> 00:00:10.934
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.934 --> 00:00:13.734
-Drugs, weed, coke? -No.

00:00:13.734 --> 00:00:14.868
-Meth? -No.

00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:16.868
-H? -No.

00:00:16.868 --> 00:00:18.000
-Acid? -No.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.367
-Molly? -No.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.000
-Are you vaping? -No.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.300
-Self-harm? -No.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.033
-Sleep too much? -No.

00:00:24.033 --> 00:00:25.834
-Too little? -No.

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:27.567
-Suicidal ideation? -[ Scoffs ]

00:00:27.567 --> 00:00:30.300
-Did you destroy any property?

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.667
-Not that they know about.

00:00:32.667 --> 00:00:34.167
-What then?

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.567
-[ Sighs ]

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:37.968
Just metal, man.

00:00:37.968 --> 00:00:41.133
-Pbbbt...

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:44.501
-Please welcome back to the show Joe Manganiello, everybody!

00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:46.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:54.100
♪♪

00:00:54.100 --> 00:01:01.868
♪♪

00:01:01.868 --> 00:01:03.501
Great to have you back. -Nice to be back.

00:01:03.501 --> 00:01:04.901
Thank you. -I want to talk about the film,

00:01:04.901 --> 00:01:06.734
but I always like connecting with you

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:08.067
over our love of Pittsburgh sports.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.234
-Yeah.

00:01:09.234 --> 00:01:10.334
-You are throwing out the first pitch.

00:01:10.334 --> 00:01:11.601
-Opening day, 4/12.

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:14.534
The area code in Pittsburgh is 412.

00:01:14.534 --> 00:01:17.100
So on 4/12, at 4:12 in the afternoon,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.501
I'm going to throw the first pitch.

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:19.667
-This is very exciting.

00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.234
Have you thrown out a first pitch before?

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:23.334
-No. I've been invited a bunch of times.

00:01:23.334 --> 00:01:25.167
I was also invited by the Dodgers.

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.767
Listen. I told them

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:28.601
I have to throw the first pitch for the Pirates first.

00:01:28.601 --> 00:01:30.334
-That's good. And I think that those are

00:01:30.334 --> 00:01:31.834
the opposite ends of the spectrum,

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:34.033
as far as what kind of season they're expecting,

00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:35.400
the Dodgers and the Pirates.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.534
-Salary cap, yes, of course.

00:01:37.534 --> 00:01:38.868
-That really speaks to your loyalty.

00:01:38.868 --> 00:01:40.934
This is a great sacrifice to the quality of play

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.634
that you might expect to see on opening day.

00:01:42.634 --> 00:01:43.834
-I am a good sport.

00:01:43.834 --> 00:01:45.734
It's going to be written on my gravestone.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:47.567
-I know you played a lot of sports growing up.

00:01:47.567 --> 00:01:50.834
Did you play baseball? -I did when I was younger

00:01:50.834 --> 00:01:53.834
until I got hit in the face with a baseball bat.

00:01:53.834 --> 00:01:56.467
-Now, it's interesting, because that doesn't happen that often.

00:01:56.467 --> 00:01:58.334
[ Laughter ] Either the guy with the bat

00:01:58.334 --> 00:02:00.367
or the guy with the face is doing something wrong.

00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:03.534
-You know, when you're a kid, what's the number-one thing?

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:05.601
Don't swing a bat when anyone's around you.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:07.100
-That's true. That's what they tell you.

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.234
-What did the kid do? Bang. And my front teeth blasted out.

00:02:09.234 --> 00:02:11.133
-Really? -So I'm actually --

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.300
This is actually a bridge I have in right now,

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.901
because after 36 years, we had to go in, like,

00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:20.067
clear out what was left of those broken teeth.

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.601
-Wow! -And drill screws into

00:02:21.601 --> 00:02:23.033
my jawline to put the cap on.

00:02:23.033 --> 00:02:25.501
-Do you think that kid has any understanding

00:02:25.501 --> 00:02:28.300
as an adult now of whose mouth he [bleep] with?

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:31.834
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:33.834
-And it wasn't cheap, all this dental work.

00:02:33.834 --> 00:02:35.100
-No, I would imagine.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:36.634
-So it's been a lifetime of that.

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:38.901
But there is a silver lining.

00:02:38.901 --> 00:02:42.467
When I was in drama school, end of freshman year,

00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:45.000
they gave me the scene of this character

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.267
who played Frankenstein on stage and complained

00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:50.734
about having this chipped tooth and he had syphilis

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:54.000
and was crazy, as you do in drama school.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.534
And so I just got a loaf of French bread

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:59.000
and worked the front tooth out in one of them.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.868
-Wow! -And I thought, well,

00:03:00.868 --> 00:03:02.667
he's Frankenstein, he's an animated corpse.

00:03:02.667 --> 00:03:06.334
So I just shaved my head bald. -Yeah.

00:03:06.334 --> 00:03:09.734
-But I didn't tell my scene partner, which, in hindsight,

00:03:09.734 --> 00:03:12.200
I probably should've said, "Hey, I'm going to do

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.901
this big, radical physical transformation for the role."

00:03:15.901 --> 00:03:19.000
And so I'm standing backstage, she's delivering a monologue.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.300
That morning I wore a ski hat

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.868
and Super Poligrip'd my tooth in so nobody knew.

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:27.434
During morning yoga warm-ups, no one had any idea.

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:30.534
And then she had this beautiful monologue,

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:32.534
and then I walk out on stage

00:03:32.534 --> 00:03:34.567
with this tooth gone and the bald head.

00:03:34.567 --> 00:03:38.267
And she went up on her lines and forgot her lines.

00:03:38.267 --> 00:03:40.234
-Yeah. Rightfully so.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.834 --> 00:03:44.667
-They were, like, talking about throwing me out of school.

00:03:44.667 --> 00:03:45.868
They were, like, "Grow your hair back.

00:03:45.868 --> 00:03:47.667
Put your tooth back in. Come back next year.

00:03:47.667 --> 00:03:49.334
Stop it. Stop it."

00:03:49.334 --> 00:03:50.667
-I do feel like the acting teacher

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:52.067
has to address the class and be like,

00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:53.367
"We don't feel like any of you

00:03:53.367 --> 00:03:55.934
should ever feel the need to remove a tooth."

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:59.200
-"Stop ripping your teeth out." Yeah.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.067
-One last thing before we move on to the movie.

00:04:01.067 --> 00:04:04.367
You are doing an Audible. You're doing the voice.

00:04:04.367 --> 00:04:06.100
You're the -- I guess the narrator is the correct way

00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.501
to describe this, about Sidney Crosby,

00:04:08.501 --> 00:04:11.100
the great Pittsburgh Penguin, about his rookie season.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.400
Now, I would imagine you've met Sidney over the years?

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:15.767
-Yeah, yeah. We've become friends over the years.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:18.501
So becoming friends with Sid and then being selected

00:04:18.501 --> 00:04:23.834
to narrate the story of his rookie season was such an honor.

00:04:23.834 --> 00:04:27.133
And, you know, I hosted the NHL Awards at one point.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:28.734
-That's cool. It's really nice.

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:30.701
When they know that you respect what they do,

00:04:30.701 --> 00:04:32.501
it makes it a lot easier, too. -I love them.

00:04:32.501 --> 00:04:35.067
I love Pittsburgh, so, you know. -That's great.

00:04:35.067 --> 00:04:37.634
This film is a lovely film

00:04:37.634 --> 00:04:40.601
about this group of teenagers who is into metal.

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:42.834
And, you know, that's a scene where you realize

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:45.367
a lot of people get worried when kids are into metal,

00:04:45.367 --> 00:04:47.434
but it's also a wonderful community

00:04:47.434 --> 00:04:49.300
and I think that sometimes adults overreact to it.

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.968
Were you into metal as a kid? -I was. Yeah.

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:55.167
And, well, when I grew up,

00:04:55.167 --> 00:04:57.701
it was, Satan is going to come for you!

00:04:57.701 --> 00:05:00.300
-Yeah. -And even though Ozzy

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.334
was accused of being a satanist, but it was like,

00:05:02.334 --> 00:05:05.367
all of his songs are about running away from Satan.

00:05:05.367 --> 00:05:08.901
Well, then you get Slayer, but that's a whole other thing.

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:10.868
But, yeah, I was.

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:13.200
My father had a great record collection,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:16.601
so I think it probably started out with Zeppelin, Doors,

00:05:16.601 --> 00:05:18.868
and then I got into, like, Sabbath.

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:23.033
My first concert was Pantera, Sepultura, Biohazard.

00:05:23.033 --> 00:05:24.434
Am I speaking a foreign language or --

00:05:24.434 --> 00:05:25.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:27.934
Okay. Yeah, cool. -So, your wife, Sofia Vergara,

00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:29.667
does she care about metal at all?

00:05:29.667 --> 00:05:31.400
-No. No, no, no. -Yeah.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:34.133
-When I go down -- I have a home gym that I built.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.300
And when I go and work out, I'll, you know,

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:39.100
put on some metal, aggressive music,

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.467
and I'll come upstairs,

00:05:40.467 --> 00:05:42.701
and she'll just have this look on her face like,

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.868
"You put on the devil music again."

00:05:44.868 --> 00:05:48.734
So she's on board with the whole Satan thing, I guess, too.

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:51.100
She thinks that Satan's coming for me when I work out.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.734
-We've talked about it. You mentioned you built a gym.

00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:54.367
We've talked about the other room in your house

00:05:54.367 --> 00:05:58.801
that is well-known in L.A. circles and in D&amp;D circles.

00:05:58.801 --> 00:05:59.934
This is a true thing.

00:05:59.934 --> 00:06:02.868
You host a D&amp;D night in Los Angeles.

00:06:02.868 --> 00:06:04.467
And you have a room for it, right?

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:06.834
-I do. It shares a wall with the gym,

00:06:06.834 --> 00:06:09.534
which is probably a good microcosm

00:06:09.534 --> 00:06:11.868
of my whole personality, I think.

00:06:11.868 --> 00:06:14.033
There's this gym and Olympic lifting going on,

00:06:14.033 --> 00:06:15.734
and on the other side of the wall,

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:17.934
it's Dungeons &amp; Dragons. I have a dungeon that I built.

00:06:17.934 --> 00:06:20.200
-It's a very funny thing to think about reselling one day

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:21.834
and having a real-estate agent be like,

00:06:21.834 --> 00:06:23.901
"And then this is a D&amp;D/gym."

00:06:23.901 --> 00:06:28.067
-Yep, yep. Well, when people -- Yeah.

00:06:28.067 --> 00:06:33.767
When people came to look at the house, I did think --

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:37.467
I hope that the realtor said something appropriate

00:06:37.467 --> 00:06:39.601
about why this is in the basement.

00:06:39.601 --> 00:06:42.634
But, you know, it's really -- it's something I grew up with.

00:06:42.634 --> 00:06:46.067
It was a real fountainhead of creativity for me.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:48.100
People ask me all the time, who was I in high school?

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.868
I was a three-sport captain who was on the honor roll.

00:06:51.868 --> 00:06:57.467
I was in AP physics, but I also ran these games for my friends.

00:06:57.467 --> 00:07:01.400
And it became kind of like -- I started making my own films

00:07:01.400 --> 00:07:02.834
and producing and directing and writing,

00:07:02.834 --> 00:07:04.567
and starring in my own movies with all my friends,

00:07:04.567 --> 00:07:07.501
and that was how I started acting, writing, producing.

00:07:07.501 --> 00:07:09.901
-From doing, like, D&amp;D-inspired? That's amazing.

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:11.601
-It was like running my own show

00:07:11.601 --> 00:07:13.868
every day during the summer for my friends

00:07:13.868 --> 00:07:15.567
who got to play characters in them.

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:16.934
-So this is really cool.

00:07:16.934 --> 00:07:18.634
D.B. Weiss, who is one of the co-creators

00:07:18.634 --> 00:07:20.868
with David Benioff of "Game of Thrones,"

00:07:20.868 --> 00:07:22.701
he wrote the "Metal" film that you're in.

00:07:22.701 --> 00:07:25.934
And he asked to join your game. -Sort of.

00:07:25.934 --> 00:07:29.701
Well, so, Tom Morello, guitar god, Rage Against the Machine,

00:07:29.701 --> 00:07:33.167
he did all the music for "Metal Lords."

00:07:33.167 --> 00:07:35.300
Tom was in my group first. -Got it.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.434
-Because I had heard that Tom played when he was a kid,

00:07:37.434 --> 00:07:41.033
and I said, "I play as an adult. It's a whole different thing.

00:07:41.033 --> 00:07:42.734
You gotta come over." [ Laughter ]

00:07:42.734 --> 00:07:47.000
So he came and played, and the group was really big.

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:48.601
-What's big?

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.934
-Big was like, we were around, like, seven, eight-ish.

00:07:50.934 --> 00:07:52.200
-Okay, gotcha.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.667
-You want to try to keep it around four to six.

00:07:53.667 --> 00:07:55.167
But we were getting into seven, we were getting into eight,

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:56.267
and Tom said, "Hey, there's this guy

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:59.400
in my kid's carpool, he wants to play."

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.033
I said, "Nah, the group's kind of big. We're ballooning.

00:08:02.033 --> 00:08:03.667
I just want to keep it intimate."

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:05.868
And he said, "He created 'Game of Thrones.'"

00:08:05.868 --> 00:08:09.100
And I went, "Oh. Dan or Dave?"

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.434
He said, "Dan." I go, "Yeah, he can play."

00:08:11.434 --> 00:08:15.033
And then Dan came and played, and then he was texting,

00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:18.567
which you don't do while the Dungeon Master is working.

00:08:18.567 --> 00:08:20.300
-Right. Of course. -And so he's texting.

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:23.467
And I'm, like, "Dude, what's up?"

00:08:23.467 --> 00:08:25.000
And he's, like, "No, it's Dave.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:26.901
He wants to know how many hit points the dragon has."

00:08:26.901 --> 00:08:28.133
I'm, like, "I'm not gonna tell you.

00:08:28.133 --> 00:08:30.100
You have to kill it, then I'll tell you."

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:32.434
And he said, "Oh, he said he's going to divorce me

00:08:32.434 --> 00:08:34.868
as a creative partner unless I get him into your game.

00:08:34.868 --> 00:08:36.234
Can he come next week?"

00:08:36.234 --> 00:08:38.234
And I said, "Alright, sure." -So you made an exception.

00:08:38.234 --> 00:08:40.801
Now, is it higher stakes for you as a Dungeon Master?

00:08:40.801 --> 00:08:43.033
Because you're running the show, right, every week?

00:08:43.033 --> 00:08:46.901
-Yeah. I'm like Dan and Dave. -For "Game of Thrones."

00:08:46.901 --> 00:08:49.801
Do they -- I'm wondering, one,

00:08:49.801 --> 00:08:51.467
what's the stress like for you with them there?

00:08:51.467 --> 00:08:53.834
And, two, are they just so relieved not to be in charge

00:08:53.834 --> 00:08:55.334
and get to just be playing?

00:08:55.334 --> 00:08:58.033
-I think the latter, because they've said it.

00:08:58.033 --> 00:09:01.534
Dan has said it a lot that he would live for the game,

00:09:01.534 --> 00:09:04.033
and if he had to miss a game, like, for a wedding,

00:09:04.033 --> 00:09:06.067
he's texting trying to find out what's going on,

00:09:06.067 --> 00:09:11.801
and everyone's like, "Dan, we're facing incredible odds."

00:09:11.801 --> 00:09:15.701
But, yeah, I think that it became this kind of fun thing

00:09:15.701 --> 00:09:17.300
where they didn't have to run the show.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.200
They could just be actors in the show that I was running.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.467
-It's about time. They deserve it.

00:09:20.467 --> 00:09:22.100
After everything they gave us, they deserve that.

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.868
So thank you for being the one to give that to them.

00:09:23.868 --> 00:09:25.667
-And I actually used their dragons against them.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:27.334
I found the figurines.

00:09:27.334 --> 00:09:29.501
My nephew gave me -- My nephew Rafa gave me

00:09:29.501 --> 00:09:31.534
a bunch of dragons that were "Game of Thrones" dragons,

00:09:31.534 --> 00:09:34.000
so I would use them and try to kill them.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.534
-Turnabout is fair play. -It is.

00:09:36.534 --> 00:09:38.767
-I know that you're incredibly proud of the work

00:09:38.767 --> 00:09:41.434
you've done over the years, but we also did some digging

00:09:41.434 --> 00:09:43.868
and saw that you were in a telenovela,

00:09:43.868 --> 00:09:45.534
but an American telenovela, right?

00:09:45.534 --> 00:09:47.501
-Yes. -What was the name of it?

00:09:47.501 --> 00:09:49.934
-Oh, boy. It was called "American Heiress."

00:09:49.934 --> 00:09:51.267
-Okay.

00:09:51.267 --> 00:09:52.701
-So, Rupert Murdoch bought all of the telenovelas

00:09:52.701 --> 00:09:54.834
from Latin America and had them translated,

00:09:54.834 --> 00:09:56.968
not written, just --

00:09:56.968 --> 00:09:59.734
-Like Google translated? -Like, you know,

00:09:59.734 --> 00:10:01.968
"How is your sister's love interest?"

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:05.701
You know, you're like... "You mean boyfriend?"

00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:08.267
[ Laughter ]

00:10:08.267 --> 00:10:10.434
And so there were all these fights that happened on set.

00:10:10.434 --> 00:10:12.801
There was no show runner. So it was like "Lord of the Flies."

00:10:12.801 --> 00:10:14.267
We were all at each other's throats

00:10:14.267 --> 00:10:15.701
arguing over what we were going to shoot.

00:10:15.701 --> 00:10:17.601
-But you shot a lot, right?

00:10:17.601 --> 00:10:19.400
Because it's, like, soap-opera schedule.

00:10:19.400 --> 00:10:21.634
-Yeah. We shot like 55 pages a day.

00:10:21.634 --> 00:10:23.367
Which, like, for example,

00:10:23.367 --> 00:10:25.567
"True Blood," we'd shoot five or six.

00:10:25.567 --> 00:10:27.834
-Wow! -Okay, so they would do

00:10:27.834 --> 00:10:31.400
everything from one side, thinking they were saving time.

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:35.167
So you would shoot 16 scenes from 11 different episodes

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:37.434
from the entire run of 65.

00:10:37.434 --> 00:10:38.501
[ Laughter ] One way.

00:10:38.501 --> 00:10:40.167
-So if your character was in a kitchen,

00:10:40.167 --> 00:10:42.300
every time he was in a kitchen for the whole season,

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:43.968
you would shoot in one day? -You'd shoot it, yeah.

00:10:43.968 --> 00:10:45.534
And then you would turn around at lunch

00:10:45.534 --> 00:10:47.367
to go the other way and get their coverage.

00:10:47.367 --> 00:10:49.334
So it was, like -- It was insane.

00:10:49.334 --> 00:10:50.934
And then, like, for example,

00:10:50.934 --> 00:10:53.734
if you were walking down a hallway

00:10:53.734 --> 00:10:55.434
and then you open the door and you walk into a room

00:10:55.434 --> 00:10:58.801
and say something, they would film the hallway

00:10:58.801 --> 00:11:01.601
two months before the room you walked into.

00:11:01.601 --> 00:11:05.634
So I kind of had an idea

00:11:05.634 --> 00:11:07.834
that they were never gonna run these episodes.

00:11:07.834 --> 00:11:09.267
-They were never gonna be on TV?

00:11:09.267 --> 00:11:11.767
-Yeah, yeah. I got a strong inkling.

00:11:11.767 --> 00:11:13.434
And so I just tried to keep myself sane.

00:11:13.434 --> 00:11:16.634
And I would go to the movie theater and give, like, a P.A.

00:11:16.634 --> 00:11:17.968
my number and just be, like,

00:11:17.968 --> 00:11:19.801
"Just hit me if you need me back.

00:11:19.801 --> 00:11:22.667
I'll be right down the street at the movie theater."

00:11:22.667 --> 00:11:24.567
And then I went --

00:11:24.567 --> 00:11:27.534
Knowing that there's a hallway scene

00:11:27.534 --> 00:11:29.267
and then I open the door to the room,

00:11:29.267 --> 00:11:31.767
we were going to film in two months later,

00:11:31.767 --> 00:11:37.300
I started heavily tanning in those two months

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:40.667
and started avoiding my haircuts on Friday

00:11:40.667 --> 00:11:44.767
and kind of wasn't shaving as much leading up in that week.

00:11:44.767 --> 00:11:48.067
And so what you got, which no one saw,

00:11:48.067 --> 00:11:51.767
was me walking down a hallway, pasty, clean-shaven,

00:11:51.767 --> 00:11:54.634
you know, like, very bright white.

00:11:54.634 --> 00:11:57.501
And I opened the door, and it's like super tan,

00:11:57.501 --> 00:11:59.901
shaggy hair, "Hey,"

00:11:59.901 --> 00:12:01.767
close the door and go right back down the hallway.

00:12:01.767 --> 00:12:03.534
-Now, I think people would've watched that show.

00:12:03.534 --> 00:12:05.534
I think you're selling it short.

00:12:05.534 --> 00:12:07.534
-You know, I tried to make it interesting.

00:12:07.534 --> 00:12:09.767
If they were ever going to make all those episodes.

00:12:09.767 --> 00:12:11.801
-Well, I'm sorry we didn't get a chance to see that,

00:12:11.801 --> 00:12:13.667
but of course it's always great to see you here.

00:12:13.667 --> 00:12:15.634
Thank you so much, Joe. -Appreciate it. Thank you.

00:12:15.634 --> 00:12:16.968
-Joe Manganiello, everybody.

00:12:16.968 --> 00:12:19.467
"Metal Lords" premieres on Netflix this Friday.

